WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.880
Hi, I'm Sandra, from HEARa.
Welcome to Day 5 of our  

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.500
10-day Lip reading Challenge,
designed to help you develop and  

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.046
practice your lip reading skills!

4
00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:16.675
Hearing loss can have a profound impact on your life.

5
00:00:16.675 --> 00:00:18.660
It touches every interaction.

6
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.098
It can affect your career, your relationships,

7
00:00:22.098 --> 00:00:23.220
and your happiness.

8
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.440
Join me to explore tips and 
tricks on this YouTube channel 

9
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:34.500
to bring confidence and clarity
to relationships in all areas of your life. 

10
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.778
Today is all about vowels.

11
00:00:37.778 --> 00:00:43.200
Vowels are useful to learn for two reasons:

12
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.149
the first is that there's a

13
00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:47.893
vowel in every word in English,

14
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.463
so if we learn more about them,

15
00:00:51.463 --> 00:00:52.950
we can increase our speech reading

16
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.047
skills exponentially!

17
00:00:56.103 --> 00:00:59.734
The second reason is that 

18
00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:01.008
it's mostly the vowels

19
00:01:01.008 --> 00:01:02.940
that give rise to accents.

20
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.580
For example, 

21
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.800
American English and British English
have all the same consonants. 

22
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.060
It's the vowel sounds
that make them sound different. 

23
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.600
Many people with hearing loss
have difficulty with accents. 

24
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.359
There is a key difference
between vowels and consonants 

25
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.655
when it comes to lip reading.

26
00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:32.576
A rule to remember is that the mouth

27
00:01:32.577 --> 00:01:35.580
and throat are open for vowel sounds.

28
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.219
Consonant sounds have

29
00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:40.860
some kind of closure or constriction.

30
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.452
For example, in the consonant sounds

31
00:01:44.453 --> 00:01:46.080
that we've learned thus far,

32
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.646
P,B, and M in Day 1 and 2,

33
00:01:49.646 --> 00:01:52.620
the lips close and then open.

34
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.719
And for the F and V sounds

35
00:01:56.719 --> 00:01:58.866
in Day 3 and 4, there was a

36
00:01:58.866 --> 00:01:59.820
constriction between the teeth and the lip.

37
00:02:00.362 --> 00:02:03.075
between the teeth and the lip.

38
00:02:03.075 --> 00:02:06.576
The air has to pass over that constriction

39
00:02:06.576 --> 00:02:08.243
[prolonged F sound].

40
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.900
And even the hard C or the K sound,
which is invisible, 

41
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.650
has the constriction at the back of the throat.

42
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.019
Try saying a K sound

43
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.573
and have that constriction

44
00:02:21.573 --> 00:02:25.040
at the back of your throat. [K]

45
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.330
So vowels are open,

46
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:32.047
and consonants have some kind of closure or constriction.

47
00:02:32.047 --> 00:02:35.476
Another thing to know about vowels is

48
00:02:35.477 --> 00:02:41.040
that they're categorized as
being short, long or diphthongs.

49
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:51.480
Short vowels are marked by a breve,
which is a smiley above the vowel. 

50
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.400
They are short because they're said quickly,
and they're quite difficult to see at times. 

51
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.439
Long vowels are marked by a straight line 

52
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.200
over the vowel,
called a macron. 

53
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.819
Long vowels are said
the same way we pronounce them 

54
00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:13.380
in the alphabet A, E, I, O, and U.

55
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.940
Long vowels are easier to prolong.

56
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.888
Diphthongs are a union of two vowels.

57
00:03:23.888 --> 00:03:28.684
An example is the oi sound in boy.

58
00:03:28.684 --> 00:03:31.483
Boy definition, uh,

59
00:03:31.483 --> 00:03:34.620
by definition,

60
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.771
diphthongs are long vowels

61
00:03:36.772 --> 00:03:39.420
because they contain two vowel sounds.

62
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.099
Let's make sure you know
the difference between 

63
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.618
short and long vowels.

64
00:03:47.618 --> 00:03:49.560
For the vowel A,

65
00:03:50.220 --> 00:04:01.560
A is long in apron and whale.
A is short in black and apple. 

66
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.200
As we go through these,
try to notice that 

67
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.067
the short vowels are quite difficult to see

68
00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:12.600
because they're passing by so quickly.

69
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.903
For the vowel E,

70
00:04:15.904 --> 00:04:19.860
E is long in easel and key.

71
00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.735
E is short in egg and effort.

72
00:04:25.735 --> 00:04:28.379
For the vowel I,

73
00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:39.120
it's long in ice and bright.
It's short in itch and six. 

74
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:47.879
For the vowel O,
O is long in overalls and boat. 

75
00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.100
O is short in octopus and fox. 

76
00:04:53.760 --> 00:05:00.180
For the vowel U,
it's long in uniform and blue. 

77
00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:05.546
U is short in umbrella and fun.

78
00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.154
Let's practice.

79
00:05:08.154 --> 00:05:12.255
I'll say a word and you tell me
if it's short or long. 

80
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.180
I'm also going to use this opportunity
to review invisible sounds 

81
00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.914
and introduce a new one.

82
00:05:20.914 --> 00:05:22.140
Are you ready?

83
00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.482
Short or long?

84
00:05:26.482 --> 00:05:27.240
Ape

85
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:30.960
Ape is long

86
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.375
The next one is cap.

87
00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.167
Cap is short.

88
00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:44.220
Cap looks like app,

89
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.073
because the hard C sound

90
00:05:47.073 --> 00:05:50.700
is invisible at the beginning of the word.

91
00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.954
Combine that with a short vowel,

92
00:05:53.954 --> 00:05:56.820
and cap is pretty hard to speech read!

93
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.450
Short or long?

94
00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:01.620
Eggnog

95
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.800
Both vowels in eggnog are short. 

96
00:06:08.407 --> 00:06:10.438
They also have an invisible G

97
00:06:10.438 --> 00:06:14.278
at the end of egg and nog,

98
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.300
so a short vowel plus invisible sounds 

99
00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.020
makes another word
that's difficult to speech read! 

100
00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.139
Short or long?

101
00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.797
Night

102
00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:31.500
The I sound in night is long.

103
00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.979
Short or long?

104
00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:36.960
YouTube

105
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.980
Both “U”s in YouTube 

106
00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.212
are long.

107
00:06:42.212 --> 00:06:48.637
The last short or long example is a phrase:

108
00:06:48.637 --> 00:06:50.280
the red fox

109
00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.886
This phrase is full of short vowels.

110
00:06:55.886 --> 00:06:59.879
It also has a new invisible sound, the X.

111
00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.720
The X sound is actually made of two sounds,

112
00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.640
the K and the S.

113
00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.580
Try it now. 

114
00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.847
Say the letter X to yourself

115
00:07:14.847 --> 00:07:17.759
and separate out the K and the S.

116
00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.760
[X]

117
00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:24.552
The S is visible,

118
00:07:24.552 --> 00:07:28.097
the K is not visible.

119
00:07:28.097 --> 00:07:31.259
So you don't see the X, 

120
00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.611
you see the S. 

121
00:07:34.162 --> 00:07:36.162
[Fox]

122
00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:45.720
So make sure you add X=K + S
to your list of invisible sounds.

123
00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:53.400
Because the short vowels
are so difficult to recognize and see, 

124
00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.820
we're actually not going 
to study them any further. 

125
00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.380
If you have difficulty with a word or a phrase 

126
00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:02.760
it's likely going to be

127
00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.680
when there's short vowels in it.

128
00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.680
There's not much you can do about it

129
00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.000
except be aware of it.

130
00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.427
That's why we did the long and short practice.

131
00:08:13.428 --> 00:08:15.840
And now for a communication tip.

132
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:22.199
If you have difficulty hearing and seeing someone  

133
00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.047
even after they repeated it,

134
00:08:25.047 --> 00:08:27.812
you could ask them to rephrase,

135
00:08:27.812 --> 00:08:31.380
instead of saying “What?” a second time.

136
00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.719
Potentially the original word or phrase

137
00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.209
had short vowels in it and maybe some invisible sounds.

138
00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.111
And then when they rephrase it, it might

139
00:08:44.111 --> 00:08:47.520
have long vowels and be easier for you to see.

140
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.485
I'll give you an example.

141
00:08:51.485 --> 00:08:55.339
Watch me as I say a sentence silently.

142
00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:56.760
Are you ready?

143
00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:04.620
Pardon?

144
00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.660
Hmm, I still didn't get it. 

145
00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.780
Can you say it a different way?

146
00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:22.620
Absolutely, I'd be happy to.

147
00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:34.727
The original sentence was:

148
00:09:34.727 --> 00:09:37.080
I like your picture of a red fox.

149
00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.493
And the rephrase was,

150
00:09:41.493 --> 00:09:45.600
I love your photo of the red fox,

151
00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.988
so I changed “like” to “love,”

152
00:09:49.989 --> 00:09:53.400
and emphasized it just a bit more.

153
00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:55.752
And then I changed

154
00:09:55.752 --> 00:09:57.269
the word picture,

155
00:09:57.269 --> 00:10:00.701
which has two vowels that are short,

156
00:10:00.701 --> 00:10:01.979
to photo,

157
00:10:01.979 --> 00:10:06.420
which has two vowels that are long.

158
00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.740
Plus you had an actual photo 
for context. 

159
00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.620
Did that make it easier for you?

160
00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.020
I'll do it again

161
00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.300
Here's another example of rephrasing. 

162
00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.500
Instead of saying,

163
00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:34.380
Seconds on the cheddar, if possible

164
00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.222
I could rephrase to:

165
00:10:37.222 --> 00:10:40.320
More cheese, please!

166
00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.079
Those long vowels are easier

167
00:10:43.079 --> 00:10:45.647
to prolong and emphasize.

168
00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.581
I have a question for you.

169
00:10:48.581 --> 00:10:52.500
How would you ask someone to rephrase?

170
00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.089
We all have different ways of saying things,

171
00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:00.000
so what works for me might not work for you.

172
00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:04.920
I do think your delivery is important: 

173
00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.609
the way you say something

174
00:11:07.609 --> 00:11:09.393
and the words that you use

175
00:11:09.393 --> 00:11:11.248
when you're asking someone

176
00:11:11.248 --> 00:11:12.959
to do something for you.

177
00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:16.411
Give it some thought,

178
00:11:16.411 --> 00:11:19.088
and let me know in the comments.

179
00:11:19.088 --> 00:11:21.180
Okay that's it for today!

180
00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.860
Tomorrow, we'll get practice 
lip reading long vowels. 

181
00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.280
In the meantime,
please like my video, 

182
00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:34.380
subscribe to my channel,
and especially share this with others. 

183
00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:39.180
Sometimes it takes a little bit of effort
to sign into Google 

184
00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:44.520
in order to be able to like and subscribe,
and I appreciate that effort. 

185
00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:48.600
That's what makes free content
go around on YouTube. 

186
00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.020
Literally.
The more people who like 

187
00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:56.760
and subscribe and engage by commenting,
the more people who will see this  

188
00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:00.060
video in the future.
So thank you, thank you, 

189
00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:03.000
Thanks for your help.
Bye!